.TH CCOL_LOG_ERROR 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
clog_error \- emit a log line at ERROR level with a backtrace
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

clog_error(clog lg, const char *fmt, ...);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Writes a log line at CLOG_ERROR level, capturing __FILE__, __LINE__
and __func__ by itself.  After the structured log line it appends a
stack backtrace as continuation lines indented by a tab:

  ts=... level=ERROR src=file.c:42 func=handle msg="db timeout"
  \et#0 ./myapp(handle+0x1a) [0x7f...]
  \et#1 ./myapp(main+0x42) [0x7f...]

A continuation line does not start with "ts=", so a log aggregator can
group it with the ERROR record before it.

The library skips backtrace frames 0 and 1, which belong to the logger
itself, so frame #0 is the caller of clog_error.  The depth of a
backtrace is capped at 64 frames.

A backtrace needs the binary to be linked with -rdynamic, so that the
library can resolve symbols.  The capture itself can fail, on a
platform with no execinfo.h or on a transient allocation failure while
the library resolves symbol names.  The logger then writes the log line
normally and appends one "\et#error backtrace unavailable"
continuation line in place of the usual lines for each frame, so the
omission is visible instead of silent.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
lg     Logger handle (clog).
fmt    printf-style format string.
\&...    Format arguments (optional).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
clog_error does NOT stop the process; use clog_fatal when you
want the process to stop.
The shared write lock also protects the write of the backtrace, so the
backtrace and the log line before it always appear together, even when
several threads log at the same time.
When memory pressure lasts for a time, or the content of a record
leaves little room before the internal buffer cap, a single backtrace
frame that the library cannot format is omitted, while a shorter frame
that follows it can still go out.  The library never writes an
incomplete frame, so every line that it writes is a complete,
well-formed record.  When not one frame fits, one "\et#error
backtrace unavailable" continuation line takes their place; this is the
same marker that the library uses when the capture itself fails.  Under
such pressure in CLOG_FMT_JSON mode the library can fail to embed the
backtrace at all, and a "bt_error":"unavailable" key then takes the
place of "bt", so the omission is visible and not silent.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
clog_error(lg, "failed to open %s: %s", path, strerror(errno));
clog_error(lg, "unexpected state %d in FSM", state);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_alert (3),
.BR clog_fatal (3),
.BR clog_warn (3),
.BR clog_open_fd_mp (3)
